    "content": "The 25th Amendment, ratified in 1967, provides clear procedures for filling a vice presidential vacancy and handling presidential incapacity or death, ensuring continuity in the U.S. executive branch. It addresses succession (Section 1), filling vice president vacancies (Section 2), voluntary transfer of power (Section 3), and involuntary removal by the Cabinet (Section 4). Key details regarding the 25th Amendment: Purpose & History: Ratified following President John F. Kennedy's assassination to clarify ambiguities in Article II regarding presidential inability and succession, as outlined in the Library of Congress – Constitution Annotated. Library of Congress – Constitution Annotated Section 1 (Succession): Confirms the Vice President becomes President if the President dies, resigns, or is removed, says LII | Legal Information Institute. LII | Legal Information Institute Section 2 (Vacancy): Allows the President to nominate a new Vice President, subject to Congressional confirmation. Section 3 (Voluntary Transfer): The President may declare in writing they are unable to perform duties, making the Vice President Acting President until a written declaration of recovery is made. Section 4 (Involuntary Removal): The Vice President and a majority of the Cabinet can declare the President unable to serve, transferring power to the Vice President, according to The National Constitution Center. The National Constitution Center Historical Usage: Used in 1973 (Ford replaced Agnew), 1974 (Ford became President, Rockefeller became Vice President), and temporary transfers for medical procedures (e.g., President Reagan, President Bush). Benefits and Application Stability: It ensures a continuous, functioning government, preventing power vacuums during health crises or disability. Accountability: Provides a democratic, legal pathway for managing an incapacitated leader. Process: Section 4 ensures that if the President contests their removal, Congress has the authority to decide the issue.",
